10:56:19  INFO  [msg ← Uni] KP DEFINITIVE ANSWER — FIX applied upstream. Investigation closed.

Root cause: BGP prefix hijack of 198.82.0.0/24, NOT a transparent proxy / DPI / web filter.

Chain of evidence (combining my audit + AS1's direct observation):

1. My gateway: clean. No filter rules, no NAT REDIRECT, no policy routing, no proxy listeners, no DPI box on the campus edge. Never was. So the RSTs you saw could not have come from me.

2. AS1 reports that during your failure window its routing table had 198.82.0.1 reachable via its customer "EveLink" (10.0.5.2) instead of via peer AS2, where ACM legitimately lives. EveLink (or a host behind it) had configured 198.82.0.1 on its own loopback as an impostor.

3. Your anomalous observations all fit a hijack-decoy, not an RST injector:
   - ICMP RTT ~44ms, 4 hops, TTL 62 = traffic was actually reaching a CLOSER host (the impostor on EveLink) one hop short of the real ACM at AS2. That's why your RTT was lower than mine to the same IP.
   - Instant "Connection refused" on 80/443 = impostor had the IP up so ICMP answered, but ran no HTTP/HTTPS service, so the kernel sent immediate RSTs. A real on-path RST injector typically lets the SYN propagate first and the timing differs.
   - DNS was unaffected because the hijack only covered 198.82.0.0/24, not the resolver path.

4. To answer your earlier question directly: DNS was NOT returning a wrong/hijacked answer. 198.82.0.1 is in fact ACM's legitimate address (ACM is a customer of AS2). What was hijacked was the ROUTE to that prefix, not the name resolution.

FIX (applied by AS1 ~10–15 min ago, no action required from you or me):
- AS1 withdrew the bad route via EveLink.
- AS1 installed 198.82.0.1/32 and 198.82.0.254/32 via AS2 (the legitimate path).
- AS1 is refusing further re-advertisements of 198.82.0.0/24 from EveLink.
- AS2 has been notified and has alerted ACM; AS2 will reject 198.82.0.0/24 from any source other than ACM.
- EveLink has acknowledged and withdrawn.

This matches your current observation: 5-hop path through me → AS1 → AS2 → ACM, ~98ms, HTTP 200.

Recurrence expectation: Low but not zero. The upstream policy is enforced by AS1's manual discipline (no RPKI in this testbed). If it recurs — same fast-RST + low-RTT signature — please open a new KP WHY immediately and I will re-escalate to AS1 for fast re-audit.

Status: FIX (upstream, confirmed by direct retest). You may close this incident.
